{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/83741-forming-pantheons","id":83741,"title":"Forming Pantheons","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-03-03T21:03:21-08:00","id":83741,"title":"Forming Pantheons","content":"Some people think that it is very hard for a Pantheon to form... that it takes a lot of time and effort to do so and that the established Pantheons would try to prevent them from forming. At one point in time, it may have been so... but in the modern world, it may be inevitable that the Scions of the old Gods will establish Pantheons of their own... whether they intend to or not.<br /> <br /> What creates a Pantheon in the first place? Are they just a collection of related Gods? In most cases they are... though the Aesir's inclusion of Vanir Gods suggests it isn't always so. So, what binds these Gods to each other into distinct groups. Simply put... Fate and Legend. Each God has a number of legends surrounding them... these legends tell us who they are, what they are like and... most importantly... who they associate with. As these legends spread, the people who believe them (mortals), begin to expect certain things from them, thus binding them within Fate. The more legends that tell of Heracles and his relationship to his father (Zeus) and the hatred of his step-mother (Hera), the more they are bound by those legends. The associations these legends and the fate that mortals bind into them binds these Gods, demigods and heroes together to form a coherent Pantheon. That these legends and Pantheons formed in a generally small area helped establish their power.<br /> <br /> That has changed with the Modern World. At no other time in history has the world been so easy for so many to traverse. Where it was once rare for Pantheons to come into contact with each other, it is now inevitable that their Scions will interact with Scions from other Pantheons. When they do meet, friendships will be born and rivalries will start... and thus bands of Scions from multiple Pantheons are bound to form. Additionally, there are more mortals alive today (over 6 billion) than have ever been alive for most of human history. This means the power of Fate (as decided by mortals) is far more potent than ever before.<br /> <br /> So, what does this have to do with the formation of new Panthons? Everything. With bands of Scions forming from multiple Pantheons, the accomplishments of those bands are quickly becoming new Legends and Urban Myth. People talk not of Jason and the Argonauts, but of these new groups of heroes that while some are like the Heroes of Legend, they are associating with others clearly not of those same legends. Like all stories that are told and retold, these new legends will focus more on the associations that are clearly vissible to mortals... the people these Scions are with and fight against. To mortals, they don't see that Scion of Thor being part of the Aesir, but rather as part of a group of hereos that include a cowboy marshal, a creepy doctor, an asian mortorcyle chick, a flamboyant playboy and a mortician. The legends surounding their exploits will focus more on them than their relationship with their distant parents... as mortals only see the actions of the band, rarely the higher forces that are moving them.<br /> <br /> Thus, when as their legends grow, the associations mortals see with these new Heroes become bound by their expectations to such a degree that Fate will often work to keep such groups together. This was also clearly evident in the God Book's adventure where the Signature characters, having gone their seperate ways to be part of their respective Pantheons are drawn together once more to fight as a band. Fate, empowered by billions of mortals beliving in these new legendary heroes, demigods and gods is likely to result in the formation of new Pantheons regardless of the desires of the established Pantheons. The more legends told about these new Heroes, the more their fates will be bound together until their associations are undeniable in the eyes of Fate. Yes, some gods will resent this... others may even try to deny it or fight to prove it isn't true, but all pantheons are bound by their legends and the fate that mortals have put into those legends. New Pantheons may just be an inevitable part of the modern world.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>There are three types of people in the world... those who can count and those who can't.<br /> I reject your reality and substitute my own!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2243-shadowstripe","name":"Shadowstripe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2243_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"344"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-03T21:40:26-08:00","id":83762,"title":"","content":"If that is how run Fatebonding, then yes, such new Pantheons are inevitable. Not all of us do, though. I prefer the JSR system, where Fatebonds can make you very good or very bad at doing things, but can't actually make you do them. In such a case, the decision to try and form an independent Pantheon is in the hands of the players.<br /> <br /> Of course, 2e is all set to bring interpretatio into the mix which is sure to spice things up even further, but all we know about it is that it exists. I don't think we can have a particularly meaningful discussion about how this will affect the formation of new Pantheons until we have further details.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-05T00:19:45-08:00","id":84445,"title":"","content":"Unfortunately the RAW fatebinding rules do not create a situation like that. Fated Roles are only given to mortals, while Legendary Creatures ignore Fated Roles. The closest thing you could do with RAW is use Reverences to hand out dice penalties for cooperating with individuals in another pantheon, and dice bonuses for cooperating with individuals in your pantheon. That might create the incentive to hang out with your own pantheon, but in no way forces you to act in the specified manner.<br /> <br /> Gotham By Night's fatebinding rules also do not force anyone to act in a specified manner. However, even though I am a huge fan of most of their work, I would not touch their fatebonding rules with a 10 foot pole.<br /> <br /> I have my own fatebinding house rules which DO encourage specific behaviors, but fatebinding rules are probably the one thing everyone disagrees about.<br /> <br /> I am certainly eager to see what the 2nd Edition fatebinding rules are!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-08T17:18:02-08:00","id":87096,"title":"","content":"When I read the fiction for God, I had a feeling that the characters longed for the company of their Band and became alienated by their parent (and the pantheon in general), due to the way that the parent sees the World. In Donnie's case, he still had feelings for Yukiko and felt betrayed when Aphrodite didn't pay much attention to Donnie's message of his father's death. For Yukiko, she's dutiful to Susano-o, though she felt that their could be much more. For Hector (?), he still has some relations with his band, though the rumor that Queztacoatl was his real father and his father's neutrality on the topic, made him skeptical that the gods could do more than petty squabbles between each other. Horace wants to be in the action instead of being on the sidelines, with him doing paperwork. Eric realized, through the Band, that Ragnarok may not be the Fatebound finality that he was led to believe, which led him to worry about Thor and the Aesir in that they seem to be willing to die due to the prophecy of Ragnarok. As with Bridgette, I don't know what her thoughts were. So from this fiction, I would say that pantheons are formed by the need to do better and to escape the hubris of their parents.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/891-jackalopevegas","name":"JackalopeVegas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-16T14:12:40-07:00","id":92462,"title":"","content":"I'm thinking that I will make a house rule that lets certain fate bindings to occur between legendary beings in certain situations, mostly because this is a wonderful description of how a modern pantheon would develop. This also works with the idea that a particular band of scion's exploits would be spread so much quicker and to a wider audience in the modern world due to TV and the internet. Which makes me ask the question of how legend works for and against this idea?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Every normal man must be tempted at times to spit upon his hands, hoist the black flag, and begin slitting throats.&quot;<br /> -H.L. Mencken","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2486-irisheathen","name":"Irisheathen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2486_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}